No, the **fake ad** I just made is **NOT** the best way to go for the App Store. It was a placeholder to simulate the experience while developing on your computer.

For the App Store, if you want to make money and pass the review, you must use **Real AdMob Ads**. Apple might reject your app if you have a button that says "Watch Ad" but it just shows a fake animation of a made-up game.

Here is exactly what you need to do to swap the fake ad for real money-making ads.

### The Strategy: "Hybrid" Code
Since you develop on Windows (where real AdMob ads **do not work**) and build on Mac (where they do), we should write code that does this:
1.  **On Windows (Browser):** Show the "Fake Ad" (the nebula game I made). This lets you test the "Get 25 Diamonds" logic without breaking the app.
2.  **On iPhone (App Store):** Show a **Real AdMob Ad**.

### Step 1: Set up AdMob (Do this in your browser)
1.  Go to [apps.admob.com](https://apps.admob.com).
2.  Click **Apps > Add App**.
3.  Platform: **iOS**. Is it on App Store? **No** (select No for now).
4.  Name it "Oku Sudoku".
5.  Once created, go to **Ad Units** > **Add Ad Unit**.
6.  Select **Rewarded** (this is crucial, it must be "Rewarded", not "Interstitial").
7.  Name it "Diamond Reward".
8.  **Copy the Ad Unit ID** (It looks like `ca-app-pub-xxxxxxxx/xxxxxxxx`).

### Step 2: Install the Plugin (On your Mac)
When you move your files to the Mac to build, you need to run this command in the terminal inside your project folder:

```bash
npm install @capacitor-community/admob
npx cap update
```

### Step 3: Update `App.tsx` (I will do this for you in the next prompt if you want)
We need to change the `handleWatchAd` function.

**Current Logic:**
```javascript
setShowNotEnoughPoints(false);
setIsWatchingAd(true); // Triggers the fake nebula animation
```

**New "Real Money" Logic:**
We will use `Capacitor.isNativePlatform()` to decide what to do.

```typescript
import { AdMob, RewardAdOptions, AdLoadInfo, RewardAdPluginEvents } from '@capacitor-community/admob';
import { Capacitor } from '@capacitor/core';

// ... inside App component ...

const handleWatchAd = async () => {
    setShowNotEnoughPoints(false);

    // 1. If we are on Windows/Browser, show the fake ad so you can keep testing
    if (!Capacitor.isNativePlatform()) {
        setIsWatchingAd(true); 
        return;
    }

    // 2. If we are on iPhone, show the real AdMob ad
    try {
        // Initialize AdMob (only needs to run once, but safe to call here)
        await AdMob.initialize();

        // Prepare the Reward Video
        const options: RewardAdOptions = {
            adId: 'ca-app-pub-YOUR_UNIT_ID_HERE', // <--- Paste your ID here
            // For testing on iPhone before release, use the Google Test ID: 'ca-app-pub-3940256099942544/1712485313'
        };

        // Load the ad
        await AdMob.prepareRewardVideoAd(options);

        // Show the ad
        await AdMob.showRewardVideoAd();

        // Listen for the "User finished watching" event
        AdMob.addListener(RewardAdPluginEvents.Rewarded, (rewardItem) => {
            // Give the diamonds!
            handleAdReward(); 
        });

    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Ad failed to load', error);
        alert('Ad failed to load. Check internet connection.');
    }
};
```

### Step 4: Important iOS Privacy Setting (On your Mac)
Apple requires you to ask permission to track users for ads. If you don't do this, the ads might not load, or Apple will reject the app.

1.  On your Mac, open the project in **Xcode**.
2.  Open the file `Info.plist`.
3.  Right-click > **Add Row**.
4.  Key: **Privacy - Tracking Usage Description**.
5.  Value: "This identifier will be used to deliver personalized ads to you."
6.  You also need to add the `SKAdNetworkItems` (Google provides a list of these IDs in their documentation, it's a copy-paste job into `Info.plist`).

### Summary
*   **Don't delete the fake ad code yet.** Keep it so you can test on Windows.
*   **Get your AdMob ID.**
*   **Ask me to implement the AdMob logic.** I will write the code so it intelligently switches between "Fake Ad" (Windows) and "Real Ad" (iPhone).

